# Operation timeouts

Set `timeouts` on a resource or data source to allow more time for a supported operation. Each value covers the complete operation, including Contentful requests and eligible HTTP retries.

For example, add this attribute inside an existing `contentful_entry` resource block to allow five minutes for create and update:

{{ tffile "examples/guides/operation-timeouts/timeouts.tf" }}

## Defaults and effective deadlines

| Operation | Default | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Managed-resource create, read, update, or delete | 2 minutes | The complete supported operation |
| Data-source read, except environment readiness | 2 minutes | The complete read, including pagination |
| Environment readiness data-source read | 10 minutes | The complete readiness wait |

An earlier deadline supplied by Terraform always takes precedence, even if the configured timeout is longer.

For managed resources, apply changes to `timeouts.read` or `timeouts.delete` before relying on them during a later refresh or destroy. These operations use the values already saved in Terraform state, with a 10-second minimum for the provider deadline. Shorter configured values remain unchanged in state. A configured `create` or `update` timeout is used as written.

List resources have no configurable operation timeout. They use Terraform's deadline when one is supplied. Without a deadline, the client gives each request and its retries a 2-minute timeout; this is not a limit on the complete list operation.

## Wait for environment readiness

The [`contentful_environment` resource](../resources/environment) returns after Contentful accepts the creation request. Copying can still be in progress. Use [`contentful_environment_status_ready`](../data-sources/environment_status_ready) to wait before creating dependent resources or repointing an alias. Increase the data source's `timeouts.read` for a longer readiness wait:

{{ tffile "examples/data-sources/contentful_environment_status_ready/data-source.tf" }}

The data source checks immediately, then polls on a 15-second interval until Contentful reports `ready`. A `failed` status ends the wait with an error. Increasing the environment resource's `timeouts.create` does not extend this separate wait.

See the [environment example](../resources/environment#example-usage) for an alias that waits for a copy to finish.

## Recover after a failed write

The provider retries rate-limited requests (HTTP 429) within the operation timeout, with the same body and version preconditions. After a connection error or HTTP 5xx response, Contentful may have accepted the write. The provider stops without resending it.

Inspect the object in Contentful and review a new `terraform plan`. If you find the object created by the failed request and Terraform has no record of it, follow its resource page to import it. Retrying creation can produce a duplicate when Contentful assigns the ID.

Before importing a credential, check what import can recover. Importing a [Personal Access Token](secrets-and-state#personal-access-tokens) recovers its ID, but Contentful returns the credential only at creation. Applying an import block for an [app or webhook signing secret](secrets-and-state#importing-signing-secrets) can rotate the existing secret.

If Terraform saved a draft, follow the recovery steps for [Entry publication](../resources/entry#publication-recovery) or [Content Type activation](../resources/content_type#activation-recovery).
